/* KMTY hero globe — the export map from the trade-show film, cut down for a
   web page.

   It is a background asset: a large, slowly turning sphere filling most of
   the right of the hero, with the photograph behind it and the headline in
   front. It does not get out of the type's way — the mask, the scrim and its
   own dark body are what keep the words readable over it.

   The film's globe ran on a 1080p wall off a laptop's HDMI lead: 30k outline
   points, tube-geometry arcs that drew themselves one market at a time, and
   DOM labels tracking each landing. None of that survives contact with a web
   page, so this is the same picture built to be served:

     * three.js is tree-shaken to the nineteen symbols it uses (480KB raw,
       121KB over the wire) and is only fetched at all once the page has
       loaded, the browser is idle, the viewport is wide enough and WebGL
       actually works. A visitor on a phone never pays for it.
     * the world outline is 6.5k points instead of 30k, delta-encoded in
       tenths of a degree — a third of a degree is about a pixel on the
       largest globe this ever draws.
     * the arcs are hand-extruded tubes with a colour ramp, drawn once and
       left standing; the motion is the globe turning under them.

   Everything the globe says is already said in text a few lines away: the
   nine markets are the chip row at the foot of the hero on a phone, and the
   ticker immediately below the hero on a desktop. So the whole thing is
   aria-hidden rather than described — a hidden list of the same nine names
   would only make a screen reader read them twice — and losing it to a dead
   GPU or a blocked script costs a visitor nothing.

   No external calls — the geometry is in this file and three.js is served
   from /vendor, same as React. */
